      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Lionel Dyke &quot;Pete&quot; Parker''' ([[September 7]], [[1895]]-[[February 11]], [[1991]]) was a Canadian radio announcer.

He served overseas in [[World War I]] from [[1916]] to [[1919]].

Parker made the world's first complete play-by-play [[radio]] broadcast of a professional [[ice hockey]] game, carried by [[CKCK Radio|CKCK]] [[Regina, Saskatchewan|Regina]], [[Saskatchewan]] on [[March 14]], [[1923]]. The game was held at Exhibition Park in Regina between the Regina Capitals and the [[Edmonton, Alberta|Edmonton]] Eskimos, who were part of the old [[Western Canada Professional Hockey League]].  Edmonton beat Regina 1-0.  (It preceded by 8 days the [[March 22]], [[1923]] first broadcast by [[Hockey Night in Canada]]'s [[Foster Hewitt]].)

This broadcast was officially recognized by [[Canada's Sports Hall of Fame]] in [[1972]].  


