  <page>
    <title>One Hundred Mile House, British Columbia</title>
    <id>275576</id>
    <revision>
      <id>28565883</id>
      <timestamp>2005-11-17T04:35:48Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <ip>66.8.251.35</ip>
      </contributor>
      <comment>revert to previous</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''One Hundred Mile House'''&lt;nowiki&gt;&lt;/nowiki&gt; (or '''100 Mile House''') is a [[district municipality]] located in central [[British Columbia|British Columbia]], [[Canada|Canada]] (51°38'60.00&quot;N 121°16'48.00&quot;W).

'''One Hundred Mile House'''&lt;nowiki&gt;&lt;/nowiki&gt; was originally known as Bridge Creek.  Its origins as a [[Canada|Canadian]] settlement go back to the [[fur]] trade.  It acquired its current name during the [[Cariboo Gold Rush]] where a roadhouse existed as a resting point for travellers moving between Kamloops and Fort Alexandria (present-day [[Quesnel, British Columbia|Quesnel]]).  The roadhouse, which has since burned down, was 100 miles up the [[Gold Rush Trail]] that originated at [[Lillooet, British Columbia|Lillooet]] (the most northerly navigable point on the [[Fraser River]]).

At present, '''One Hundred Mile House'''&lt;nowiki&gt;&lt;/nowiki&gt; is the primary service centre for the South Cariboo and has a population of approximately 2,000.  The service area has a population roughly ten times the size of the town.  It includes the communities of Lac La Hache, Forest Grove, Lone Butte, Bridge Lake, 70 Mile House, and [[108 Mile Ranch, British Columbia|108 Mile Ranch]], the largest residential centre between [[Kamloops, British Columbia|Kamloops]] and [[Williams Lake, British Columbia|Williams Lake]].   The primary industries of '''One Hundred Mile House'''&lt;nowiki&gt;&lt;/nowiki&gt; are forestry and ranching.

'''One Hundred Mile House'''&lt;nowiki&gt;&lt;/nowiki&gt; is a centre for outdoor activities and is becoming increasingly known for its richness of [[bird]] life. The surrounding area features many lakes for boating and fishing including Lac La Hache, Canim Lake, Horse Lake, Green Lake, and Bridge Lake.  The Cariboo ski marathon attracts a large and international field of cross-country (Nordic) skiers.

==External link==
*[http://www.britishcolumbia.com/regions/towns/?townID=3527&amp;webregionid=2 More information and related links]

[[Category:Communities in British Columbia]]</text>
    </revision>
