  <page>
    <title>Fever Pitch</title>
    <id>275678</id>
    <revision>
      <id>35267751</id>
      <timestamp>2006-01-15T13:21:34Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Qwghlm</username>
        <id>122121</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>rm album information and split to new page; rm see also section and use {{main}} instead</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{about|the Nick Hornby book and related films|the album|[[Fever Pitch (album)]]}}
'''''Fever Pitch''''' is the title of a 1992 book by [[United Kingdom|British]] author [[Nick Hornby]]. The book is the basis for two films of the same name: a [[Fever Pitch (1997 movie)|British film]] was released in 1997, and an [[Fever Pitch (2005 movie)|American remake]] in 2005. There is also an unrelated 1985 movie ''[[Fever Pitch (1985 movie)|Fever Pitch]]'', starring [[Ryan O'Neal]].

== Book ==
''Fever Pitch'' by Nick Hornby was first published in 1992. It is [[autobiographical]], telling the story of the author's relationship with [[football (soccer)]] and with [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal Football Club]] in particular. It consists of a large number of short essays, each focused on a single match between 1968 and 1992. As well as recounting Arsenal's highs and lows, Hornby relates them to his own tumultuous personal life, musing both on his worship of Arsenal heroes such as [[Liam Brady]], and the fate of infamous failures such as [[Gus Caesar]].

''Fever Pitch'' sold over a million copies in the [[United Kingdom]]. It has been seen as something of a turning point in the public perception of football, making an interest in the game acceptable, even fashionable, in educated and literary circles.

== 1997 movie ==
{{main|Fever Pitch (1997 film)}}
A 1997 film version of ''Fever Pitch'', with a screenplay adapted by Hornby himself, fictionalised the story, concentrating on Arsenal's [[Football League First Division|First Division]] championship-winning season in [[1988-89 in English football|1988-89]] and its effect on the protagonist's romantic relationship. [[Colin Firth]] played &quot;Paul Ashworth&quot;, the character based on Hornby.

== 2005 movie ==
{{main|Fever Pitch (2005 film)}}

A 2005 film remake of ''Fever Pitch'', directed by the [[Farrelly Brothers]] with Hornby as an executive producer, starred [[Jimmy Fallon]] and [[Drew Barrymore]]. In this adaptation of the first film's fictionalized version of the book, the action is moved from [[London]] to [[Boston]], the focus of the protagonist's obsession is shifted from football to [[baseball]], and the story centers on the [[Boston Red Sox]] season in 2004 which culminated with the club's first [[Major League Baseball]] [[World Series]] [[2004 World Series|victory]] in 86 years.

{{wikiquote}}

== External links ==
*{{imdb title|id=0119114|title=Fever Pitch (1997)}}
*{{imdb title|id=0332047|title=Fever Pitch (2005)}}

[[Category:1992 books]]
[[Category:1997 films]]
[[Category:Nick Hornby novels]]
[[Category:Arsenal F.C.]]
[[Category:Football (soccer) films]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
