  <page>
    <title>Cabaret Voltaire (band)</title>
    <id>275709</id>
    <revision>
      <id>34638498</id>
      <timestamp>2006-01-10T18:14:04Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <ip>195.93.21.42</ip>
      </contributor>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Cabaret Voltaire''' is a [[post-punk]] [[industrial music|industrial]] and [[techno music|techno]] group from [[Sheffield]], [[England]] consisting of  [[Stephen Mallinder]] , [[Richard H. Kirk]] and [[Chris Watson]].  Their earliest performances were [[Dada|dadaist]]-inspired [[performance art]]. In [[1978]] (see [[1978 in music]]), Cabaret Voltaire signed to [[Rough Trade Records|Rough Trade]] and after several highly acclaimed initial singles/extended players, including &quot;Extended Play&quot;, &quot;Nag Nag Nag&quot;, ''Three Mantras'' and ''3 Crepuscule Tracks''  began releasing punk-influenced albums of experimental music such as ''The Voice of America'' in [[1980]] and the widely-hailed ''Red Mecca'' in [[1981]]. 

In [[1983]] (see [[1983 in music]]), co-inciding with the departure of Watson (who went on as part of '''[[Hafler Trio|The Hafler Trio]]'''), Cabaret Voltaire began moving towards [[techno]] with the album ''[[The Crackdown]]'' on [[Virgin Records]]. In [[1984]], the singles &quot;Sensoria&quot; and &quot;James Brown&quot; from the album ''[[Micro Phonies]]'' (also on Virgin) charted on the independent music charts. In  [[1987]] (see [[1987 in music]]), the band released ''[[Code (album)|Code]]'', followed by the [[house music|house]]-influenced ''[[Groovy, Laidback &amp; Nasty]]'' in [[1990]] (see [[1990 in music]]). A series of completely instrumental works under the Cabaret Voltaire name were released on [[Instinct Records]] in [[1993]] and [[1994]], but appeared to be largely the product of Kirk. Since then, Kirk has begun a solo career under several names, including Electronic Eye and Sandoz, while Mallinder has relocated to [[Perth, Western Australia|Perth]], [[Australia]] and records with a collaborator under the name Sassi &amp; Loco and more recently in another collaborative effort the Kuling-Bros.


==Band Members==

* [[Stephen Mallinder]] - vocals, bass (1978-1994)
* [[Richard H. Kirk]] - guitars, keyboards, tapes (1978-1994)
* [[Chris Watson]] - keyboards, tapes (1978-1983)

==Discography==

'''Singles'''
*''Talkover/Do The Mussolini'' (July [[1978]])
*''Nag Nag Nag'' (April [[1979]])
*''Silent Command'' (October [[1979]])
*''Three Mantras'' (January [[1980]])
*''Seconds Too Late'' (September [[1980]])
*''Sluggin' For Jesus/Your Agent Man'' (March [[1981]])
*''Jazz the Glass/Walls of Jericho'' (July [[1981]])
*''2x45'' (October [[1981]])
*''Walls of Kyoto/Taxi Music'' (March [[1982]])
*''Crackdown/Just Fascination'' (December [[1982]])
*''The Dream Ticket'' (September [[1983]])
*''Sensoria'' (March [[1984]])
*''James Brown'' (October [[1984]])
*''Drinking Gasoline'' (January [[1985]])
*''I Want You'' (June [[1985]])
*''Shakedown'' (March [[1986]])
*''Don't Argue'' (February [[1987]])
*''Here To Go'' (September [[1987]])
*''Hypnotised'' (December [[1989]])
*''Keep On'' (June [[1990]])
*''Easy Life'' (October [[1990]])
*''Colours'' (January [[1991]])
*''What Is Real'' (April [[1991]])
*''Percussion Force'' (September [[1991]])
*''I Want You/Kino '92'' (February [[1992]])

'''Albums'''
*''Mix-Up'' (October [[1979]])
*''The Voice of America'' (July [[1980]])
*''Red Mecca'' (August [[1981]])
*''The Crackdown'' (August [[1983]])
*''Micro-Phonies'' (November [[1984]])
*''The Covenant, The Sword and The Arm of the Lord'' (October [[1985]])
*''Code'' (October [[1987]])
*''Groovy, Laidback and Nasty'' (June [[1990]])
*''Body and Soul'' (March [[1991]])
*''Plasticity'' (October [[1992]])
*''Technology:Western Re-Works 1992'' (June [[1992]])
*''International Language'' (June [[1993]])
*''The Conversation'' (July [[1994]])

==Trivia==
In the movie [[Ferris Bueller's Day Off]] you can see a Cabaret Voltaire poster in Ferris' room. It is the cover of their 1984 release Micro-Phonies.

The audio samples from ''Don't Argue'' come from a 1945 post war American Propaganda film called [[Your Job in Germany]] directed by Frank Capra and scripted by Theodor Seuss Geisel aka Dr. Seuss.

&quot;Don't clasp that hand, It is not the type of hand, That you would care to clasp in friendship&quot;

==See also==
[[Cabaret Voltaire (Zürich)]] &amp;ndash; the original dadaist cabaret.
