  <page>
    <title>Amborella</title>
    <id>275862</id>
    <revision>
      <id>40487769</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-20T22:56:15Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Choess</username>
        <id>245519</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>authorities for remainder of classification scheme</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{Taxobox
| color = lightgreen
| name = ''Amborella''
| regnum = [[Plant]]ae
| divisio = [[flowering plant|Magnoliophyta]]
| classis = [[dicotyledon|Magnoliopsida]]
| ordo = '''Amborellales'''
| ordo_authority = Melikyan, A.V.Bobrov &amp; Zaytzeva
| familia = '''Amborellaceae'''
| familia_authority = [[Marcel Pichon|Pichon]]
| genus = '''''Amborella'''''
| genus_authority = [[Henri Ernest Baillon|Baill.]]
| species = '''''A. trichopoda'''''
| binomial = ''Amborella trichopoda''
| binomial_authority = [[Henri Ernest Baillon|Baill.]]
}}

'''''Amborella trichopoda''''' is a rare [[shrub]] found only in [[New Caledonia]]. It is of [[botany|botanical]] interest because genetic studies place it at or near the base of the [[flowering plant]]s. That is, it represents a line of flowering plants that very early on diverged from all the other extant species of flowering plants, and so gives us some ideas about what the ancestral flowering plants were like. In newer classification schemes, ''Amborella'' is given its own family and order. The older [[Cronquist system]] treated it as a family within the Order [[Laurales]].

The [[leaf|leaves]] are alternately arranged, [[evergreen]], simple, with a serrated margin, and about 8&amp;ndash;10 cm long. ''Amborella'' produces small [[flower]]s 4&amp;ndash;8 mm across in loose clusters, each flower with several spirally-arranged [[tepal]]s. ''Amborella'' is [[plant sexuality|dioecious]]: each flower produces both [[stamen]]s and [[carpel]]s, but only one sex develops fully and fertile in the flowers of an individual plant, the structures of the other sex remaining  undeveloped. The [[fruit]] is a red berry containing a single [[seed]], 5&amp;ndash;8 mm long.

Individuals of this species in the wild are being reduced by [[overgrazing]] and [[habitat destruction]].
==External links==
* [http://www.ucalgary.ca/~laidlaw/amborella/amborella_web.html National Tropical Botanical Garden (Hawaii, USA): article with detailed photos of plants in cultivation]
* [http://delta-intkey.com/angio/www/amborell.htm  Amborellaceae] in [http://delta-intkey.com/angio/ L. Watson and M.J. Dallwitz (1992 onwards). The families of flowering plants: descriptions, illustrations, identification, information retrieval.] http://delta-intkey.com

