  <page>
    <title>Flight data recorder</title>
    <id>275894</id>
    <revision>
      <id>39508574</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-14T00:10:11Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Batmanand</username>
        <id>131948</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>minor factual addition (and 1000th edit!)</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{mergedisputed|Black Box (transportation)}}

[[Image:Fdr sidefront.jpg|frame|&lt;center&gt;An example of a Flight Data Recorder&lt;/center&gt;]]
The '''flight data recorder''' ('''FDR''') is a [[flight recorder]] used to record specific [[aircraft]] performance parameters.  A separate device is the [[cockpit voice recorder]] (CVR), although some recent types combine both in one unit. Popularly, though almost always falsely, known as the [[Black Box (transportation)|black box]] used for [[accidents and incidents in aviation|aircraft mishap]] analysis, the FDR is also used to study [[air safety]] issues, [[fatigue (material)|material degradation]], and [[jet engine]] performance. These [[ICAO]] regulated &quot;black box&quot; devices are often used as an aid in investigating [[accidents and incidents in aviation|aircraft mishap]], and these devices are typically one of the highest priorities for recovery after a crash, second only to bodies of victims. The device's shroud is usually painted bright [[orange (colour)|orange]] and is generally located in the tail section of the aircraft.

==History==
The first [[prototype]] FDR was produced in 1957 by Dr. David Warren of the then Aeronautical Research Laboratories of Australia.  In 1953 and 1954, a series of [[fatal]] [[accidents and incidents in aviation|mishaps]] on the [[De Havilland Comet|de Havilland DH106 Comet]] prompted the grounding of the entire fleet pending an investigation.  Dr. Warren, a [[chemist]] specializing in aircraft fuels, was involved in a professional committee discussing the possible causes.  Since there had been no [[witness|witnesses]], and no survivors, Dr. Warren began to conceive of a crash survivable method to record the [[flight_crew|flight crew's]] conversation, reasoning they would likely know the cause.

Despite his 1954 report entitled &quot;A Device for Assisting Investigation into Aircraft Accidents&quot; and a 1957 [[prototype]] FDR named &quot;The ARL Flight Memory Unit&quot;, [[aviation]] authorities from around the [[world]] were largely uninterested. This changed in 1958 when Sir Robert Hardingham, the [[Secretary]] of the UK Air Registration Board, became interested.  Dr. Warren was asked to create a pre-production model which culminated into the &quot;Red Egg&quot;, the world's first commercial FDR by the [[United Kingdom|British]] firm, S. Davall &amp; Son. The &quot;Red Egg&quot; got its name from the [[shape]] and bright [[red]] color.  Incidentally, the term &quot;Black Box&quot; came from a meeting about the &quot;Red Egg&quot;, when afterwards a [[journalist]] told Dr. Warren, &quot;This is a wonderful black box.&quot;

==Design==
The design of today's FDR is largely governed by the [http://www.eurocae.org/ European Organisation for Civil Aviation Equipment] in its EUROCAE ED-112 (Minimum Operational Performance Specification for Crash Protected Airborne Recorder Systems).  In the United States, the [[Federal Aviation Administration]] (FAA) regulates all aspects of U.S. [[aviation]], and cites [[design]] [[requirement|requirements]] in their Technical Standard Order, [http://www.airweb.faa.gov/Regulatory_and_Guidance_Library/rgTSO.nsf/0/CDFB8415D43E695786256DAC0061EC73?OpenDocument TSO-C124a], which mostly refers back to ED-112 (like many other countries' aviation authorities).

Currently, EUROCAE specifies that a recorder must be able to withstand an acceleration of 3400 [[Gee|g]] (33 km/s&amp;sup2;) acceleration for 6.5 [[millisecond]]s.  This is roughly equivalent to an [[impact]] [[velocity]] of 270 [[Knot (speed)|knots]] and a [[deceleration]] or crushing distance of 450 [[centimeter|mm]].  Additionally, there are [[requirement|requirements]] for penetration resistance, [[Statics|static]] crush, high and low temperature [[fire]]s, deep sea [[pressure]], [[sea water]] immersion, and [[fluid]] immersion.

Modern day FDRs are typically plugged into the aircraft's [[fly-by-wire]] main [[data bus]].  They record significant [[flight]] parameters, including the control and [[actuator]] positions, engine information and time of day. There are 88 parameters required as a minimum by current U.S. federal regulations (only 29 were required until [[2002]]), but some systems monitor many more [[variable|variables]].  Generally each parameter is recorded a few times per [[second]], though some units store &quot;bursts&quot; of data at a much faster [[frequency]] if the data begins to change quickly.  Most FDRs record more than a day's worth of data.

Recently aviation authorities have begun to place FDRs in the [[empennage]].  In this position, the entire front of the [[aircraft]] acts as a &quot;crush zone&quot; to reduce the shock that reaches the recorder.  Also, modern FDRs are typically double wrapped, in strong [[corrosion]]-resistant [[stainless steel]] or [[titanium]], with high-temperature [[insulation]] inside.  Additionally, since the recorders are sometimes crushed into unreadable pieces, or never located, some modern units are self-ejecting, with [[radio]] and [[sonar]] beacons (i.e. [[emergency locator transmitter]]).

==See also==
*[[Air safety]]
*[[Emergency locator transmitter]]
*[[EPIRB]]

==External links==
===General information===
*[http://www.dsto.defence.gov.au/page/3246/ Black Box Flight Recorder] &amp;mdash; Australian Department of Defence article on the flight recorder
*[http://www.dsto.defence.gov.au/page/3383/ Dave Warren - Inventor of the black box flight recorder] &amp;mdash; Australian Department of Defence article on the flight recorder's inventor
*[http://www.howstuffworks.com/black-box.htm How Black Boxes Work] &amp;mdash; Detailed seven page article from [[HowStuffWorks]]
*[http://freshgasflow.com/flight401.htm Eastern 401- The Black Box Story] Black Box transcript description of Eastern 401 crash

===Manufacturers' websites===
*[http://www.ssfdr.com/record.html Honeywell Flight Data Recorders]
*[http://www.l-3ar.com/html/products.html L-3 Communications Corp. Aviation Recorders]
*[http://www.sfiminc.com/Products.htm SFIM Inc. Aircraft Data Recording Systems]
*[http://www.aeroplan.com/Aircraft Data Recording Systems, (broken link!)]
*[http://www.smiths-aerospace.com/products/Product_Group/default.asp?ProductGroupID=F254362A-F6F3-48D5-8B65-4A46152723D7&amp;Referer=Division Smiths Aerospace Crash Protected Recorders]

[[Category:Aircraft components]]

