  <page>
    <title>Flexible AC transmission system</title>
    <id>275935</id>
    <revision>
      <id>40552992</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-21T10:57:49Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <ip>217.194.34.108</ip>
      </contributor>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Flexible Alternating Current Transmission System''' (FACTS) is a static equipment used for the [[alternating current|AC]] [[electric power transmission|transmission of electrical energy]]. It is meant to enhance controllability and increase power transfer capability. It is generally a [[power electronics]]-based device.

FACTS is defined by the [[IEEE]] as &quot;a power electronic based system and other static equipment that provide control of one or more AC transmission system parameters to enhance controllability and increase power transfer capability.&quot;

== FACTS technology ==

FACTS could be connected:
* in series with the power system (series compensation)
* in shunt with the power system (shunt compensation)
* both in series and in shunt with the power system

=== Series compensation ===

In series compensation, FACTS is connected in series with the power system. It works as a controllable voltage source.

=== Shunt compensation ===

In shunt compensation, power system is connected in shunt with the FACTS. It works as a controllable current source.

== Examples of FACTS for series compensation ==

* Static Synchronous  Series Compensator (SSSC)
* Thyristor Controlled Series Capacitor (TCSC) or Thyristor Controlled Series Reactor (TCSR)
* Thyristor Switched Series Capacitor (TSSC) or Thyristor Switched Series Reactor (TSSR)

== Examples of FACTS for shunt compensation ==

* Static Synchronous Compensator (STATCOM)
* Static Var Compensator (SVC)
** Thyristor Controlled Reactor (TCR)
** Thyristor Switched Reactor (TSR)
** Thyristor Switched Capacitor (TSC)
** Mechanically Switched Capacitor (MSC)

== External Links ==

# [http://www.siemens.com/FACTS Siemens AG - FACTS] website.

[[Category:Electricity distribution]]
[[Category:Electrical engineering]]
[[fr:FACTS]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Phimai historical park</title>
    <id>275936</id>
    <revision>
      <id>25675572</id>
      <timestamp>2005-10-16T18:45:20Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Oabplanalp</username>
        <id>504994</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>addition of a picture</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">[[Image:Phimai Prang.jpg|200px|thumbnail|right|Main shrine]]
The '''Phimai historical park''' protects one of the most important [[Khmer empire|Khmer]] temples of [[Thailand]]. It is located in the town of [[Phimai]], [[Nakhon Ratchasima province]], {{coor dm|15|13|N|102|30|E|}}.

The temple marks one end of the [[Ancient Khmer Highway]] from [[Angkor]]. As the enclosed area of 1020x580m is comparable with that of [[Angkor Wat]], Phimai must have been an important city in the Khmer empire. Most buildings are from the late [[11th century|11th]] to the late [[12th century]], built in the Baphuon, Bayon and Angkor Wat style. However, even though the Khmer at that time were [[Hinduism|Hindu]], the temple was built as a [[Buddhism|Buddhist]] temple, as Buddhism in the Khorat area dated back to the [[7th century]]. Inscriptions name the site ''Vimayapura'' (which means city of ''Vimaya''), which developed into the Thai name ''Phimai''.

The first inventory of the ruins was done in [[1901]] by the French geographer [[Etienne Aymonier]]. They were put under governmental protection by announcement in the Government Gazette, Volume 53, section 34, from [[September 27]], [[1936]]. Most of the restorations were done from [[1964]] to [[1969]] as a joint Thai-French project. The historical park, now managed by the Fine Arts Department, was officially opened by Princess [[Maha Chakri Sirindhorn]] on [[April 12]], [[1989]].

[[Image:Phimai Lintel.jpg|400px|thumb|center|[[Lintel]] over the northern entry to main shrine, showing dancing [[Vajrasattva]]]]
[[Image:Pimai1.jpg|400px|thumb|center|passing the first surrounding wall through the main entrance]]


==References==
*Michael Freeman - A guide to Khmer temples in Thailand and Laos, ISBN 0834804506

==External link==
*http://www.phimai.ca - Computer model of the temple
*{{wikitravelpar|Phimai Historical Park}}

{{Angkorian Sites}}

[[ja:&amp;#12500;&amp;#12510;&amp;#12540;&amp;#12452;&amp;#27508;&amp;#21490;&amp;#20844;&amp;#22290;]]
[[Category:Historical parks of Thailand]]
[[Category:Isan]]</text>
    </revision>
