  <page>
    <title>Massif</title>
    <id>275972</id>
    <revision>
      <id>32253452</id>
      <timestamp>2005-12-21T17:11:16Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <ip>128.192.50.34</ip>
      </contributor>
      <text xml:space="preserve">In [[geology]], a '''massif''' is a section of the [[Earth]]'s [[Crust (geology)|crust]] that is demarcated by [[geologic fault|fault]]s or [[flexure]]s. In the movement of the crust, a massif tends to retain its internal structure while being displaced as a whole. The term is also used to refer to a group of [[mountain]]s formed by such a structure. The massif is a smaller structural unit of the crust than a [[Plate tectonics|plate]]. 

The word is taken from [[French language|French]], where it is used to refer to a large mountain mass or compact group of connected mountains forming an independent portion of a range. One of the most notable [[Europe]]an examples of a massif is the [[Massif Central]] of the [[Auvergne (province)|Auvergne]] region of [[France]].

*[[Armorican Massif]] - [[Brittany]], [[France]]
*[[Annapurna]] Massif - [[Nepal]]
*[[Atlantis Massif]] - part of the [[Mid-Atlantic Ridge]] in the [[North Atlantic Ocean]].
*[[Chartreuse Massif]] - [[France]]
*[[Kugitangtau Ridge]] - [[Turkmenistan]]
*[[Laurentian Massif]] - [[Quebec]], [[Canada]] (French: ''Massif des Laurentides'')
*[[Mount Logan|Logan Massif]] - [[Yukon]], Canada
*[[Massif Central]] - [[France]]
*[[Massif Montgris]] - [[Spain]]
*[[Panchchuli Massif]] - [[India]] 
*[[Vinson Massif]] - [[Antarctica]]
*[[Vitosha]] Massif - [[Bulgaria]]
*[[Adrar des Ifoghas]] - [[Mali]]

In [[mountaineering]] and [[climbing]] literature, a massif is frequently used to denote the main mass of an individual mountain.

[[Category:Landforms]]
[[Category:Mountains]]

[[nl:Massief]]
[[pl:Masyw g&amp;#243;rski]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>North Carolina Bicycle Route 5</title>
    <id>275973</id>
    <revision>
      <id>40483203</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-20T22:20:38Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Therealdantheman</username>
        <id>266251</id>
      </contributor>
      <text xml:space="preserve">[[Image:http://www.ncdot.org/transit/bicycle/maps/images/originals/E_cfr.gif]]'''North Carolina Bicycle Route 5''', the ''Cape Fear Run'', travels 160 miles along the [[Cape Fear River]] from [[Apex, North Carolina|Apex]] through the southeast coastal plain to [[Wilmington, North Carolina|Wilmington]] at the sea.  In Wilmington, it connects with [[North Carolina Bicycle Route 3]], the ''Ports of Call Route''.  Rolling hills soon give way to flat land in the swamps and Carolina bays typical of this region of the state.  Notable points of interest include [[Jones Lake State Park]], [[Moore's Creek National Military Park]], the [[USS North Carolina Battleship Memorial]], [[Brunswick Town State Historic Site]], [[Carolina Beach State Park]], and [[Fort Fisher State Historic Site]].
This is one of nine bicycle routes designated, signed and mapped by the North Carolina Department of Transportation Bicycle and Pedestrian Division.

==External links==
*The NC-DOT has [http://www.ncdot.org/transit/bicycle/maps/maps_highways.html#capefear route descriptions and maps].

[[Category:North Carolina outdoor recreation]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
