  <page>
    <title>Emley Moor</title>
    <id>276113</id>
    <revision>
      <id>38628252</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-07T16:17:08Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <ip>82.47.184.134</ip>
      </contributor>
      <comment>Added {{Supertall}}</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">[[image:EmleyMoorMast.JPG|thumb|right|250px|The Emley Moor tower]]

'''Emley Moor''' (located at {{coor dm|53|36|N|1|39|W|}}) is an area of [[moorland]] near the village of [[Emley]], near [[Huddersfield]] in [[West Yorkshire]], [[England]] (national grid reference: SE222128). It is the site of the [[Arqiva]]-owned Emley Moor transmitting station. Its reinforced concrete tower is the tallest free-standing structure on the [[British Isles]] at a height of 330.4 [[metre]]s (1084 [[Foot (unit of length)|feet]]). Reaching the Tower Room (at the top of the concrete part of the tower at 275 m (900 ft)) involves a seven minute journey by lift. The antenna structure above this is a further 56 m (184 ft).  This is taller than [[1 Canada Square]], Britain's tallest ''building'', which is only 244 m (800 ft) high. However, it is not as tall as the [[Belmont Transmitter]] in [[Lincolnshire]], which is a guyed mast with a height of 385 m (1265 ft), making it the tallest structure of any kind in the country. 
On the other hand, the Emley Moor tower
is taller than the [[Eiffel Tower]], which is just 300 m (984 ft)
high, with an additional 24m (79 ft) tower on top.

The top of the current tower at Emley Moor is 594 m (1949 ft) above [[sea level]]. Its foundations penetrate 6.1 m (20 ft) into the ground. The whole structure, including foundations, weighs 11200 tonnes.

The current tower is the third structure to have occupied the site. The original 135 m (445 ft) tower was erected in [[1956]] to provide [[ITV|independent television]] broadcasts to the [[Yorkshire]] area. It was replaced in 1964 by a taller 385 m (1265 ft) guyed mast (identical to the structure still standing at Belmont).

On [[19 March]] [[1969]], a combination of strong winds and the weight of ice that had formed around the top of the mast and on the guys brought the structure down. After a series of temporary masts, erection of the current [[concrete]]-built tower began in [[1969]] with transmissions commencing on [[21 January]] [[1971]]. It was designed by ''[[Ove Arup]] and Partners''.

The Emley Moor tower broadcasts [[BBC1]], [[BBC2]], [[Yorkshire Television|ITV1 Yorkshire]], [[Channel 4]], [[five (TV)|five]], six [[digital television]] multiplexes, two [[digital radio]] multiplexes and two independent local radio stations (''Galaxy 105'' and ''Real Radio'') over an area of approximately 10,000 km&amp;sup2;. It is the main station for some 51 relays and repeaters throughout Yorkshire and surrounding counties.

The UK Government declared Emley Tower a Grade II [[Listed building|Listed Building]] of 'significant architectural or historic interest' in 2002.

==Channels listed by Frequency==

* Analogue Radio ([[Frequency Modulation|FM]] [[VHF]])
** 105.1 [[Megahertz|MHz]] - [[Galaxy 105|Galaxy]]
** 106.2 MHz - [[Real Radio (Yorkshire)|Real]]

* Digital Radio ([[Digital audio broadcasting|DAB]])
** Block 11D: 222.064 MHz - [[Digital One]]
** Block 12B: 225.648 MHz - [[BBC DAB National|BBC]]

* Analogue Television
** [[UHF]] 37 (599.25 MHz) - [[Five (TV)|Five]]
** UHF 41 (631.25 MHz) - [[Channel 4]]
** UHF 44 (655.25 MHz) - [[BBC One]]
** UHF 47 (679.25 MHz) - [[ITV1]]
** UHF 51 (711.25 MHz) - [[BBC Two]]

* Digital Television
** UHF 40 (626 MHz) - Multiplex 2 [[Digital 3&amp;4]]
** UHF 43 (650 MHz) - Multiplex A [[SDN]]
** UHF 46 (674 MHz) - Multiplex B [[BBC]]
** UHF 49 (698 MHz) - Multiplex D [[National Grid Wireless]]
** UHF 50 (706 MHz) - Multiplex C [[National Grid Wireless]]
** UHF 52 (722 MHz) - Multiplex 1 [[BBC]]

